  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "type": "theory",
      "title": "The two most important verbs",
      "body": "# *Être* and *avoir*\n\nIf you only learn two French verbs at A1, learn **être** (to be) and **avoir** (to have). Together they cover:\n\n- Identity: *Je **suis** étudiant.* - I am a student.\n- Description: *Elle **est** grande.* - She is tall.\n- Possession: *J'**ai** un chat.* - I have a cat.\n- Age: *Tu **as** vingt ans.* - You are twenty (lit.: you have twenty years).\n- Plus the **passé composé**, French's main past tense, is built from *avoir* + past participle (Lesson 10).\n\nBoth verbs are **irregular**, the forms don't follow a pattern, you have to memorise them. Same in English (am / are / is / was / were), same in Spanish (soy / eres / es), same in German (bin / bist / ist). It's worth the upfront effort."
    },
    {
      "id": "step-etre",
      "type": "theory",
      "title": "Être - to be",
      "body": "## Present tense of *être*\n\n| Subject | Form |\n|---|---|\n| je | **suis** |\n| tu | **es** |\n| il / elle | **est** |\n| nous | **sommes** |\n| vous | **êtes** |\n| ils / elles | **sont** |\n\nPronunciation traps:\n- *suis*, silent **s** at the end, sounds like \"swee\"\n- *est*, the **s** and **t** are silent: just \"eh\"\n- *êtes*, the circumflex marks an old **s**: pronounced \"ett\"\n- *sont*, nasal vowel + silent **t**: \"sohn\"\n\nUse *être* for: identity, profession, nationality, description, location.\n\n*Je **suis** français. Tu **es** ici. Nous **sommes** prêts.*"
    },
