    {
      "id": "step-avoir",
      "type": "theory",
      "title": "Avoir - to have",
      "body": "## Present tense of *avoir*\n\n| Subject | Form |\n|---|---|\n| je (j') | **ai** |\n| tu | **as** |\n| il / elle | **a** |\n| nous | **avons** |\n| vous | **avez** |\n| ils / elles | **ont** |\n\nMandatory elision: *je* + *ai* always becomes **j'ai**. You'll never see *je ai*.\n\nUse *avoir* for: possession, age, certain idioms.\n\n- *J'**ai** trois enfants.* - I have three children.\n- *Vous **avez** vingt ans.* - You are twenty years old. (Literally: you HAVE twenty years.)\n- *Ils **ont** faim.* - They are hungry. (Literally: they HAVE hunger.)\n\nFrench uses *avoir* where English uses *be* for age, hunger, thirst, fear, and being right or wrong. Translate by meaning, not word-for-word."
    },
